#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115093 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd783d1a-745d-5cd7-a5c3-9a109b6d1350" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Graph of a Horizontally Stretched Function"}] :question/id #uuid "57ece1a0-70e5-523f-8f6d-73a2fef43492" :question/math-academy-id "e-4983" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a11bef66d0529418dde00e5713ed5e98f53a948c8a60f83fd056fd9e2c18698.png)\n\nPlot the graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x \\right)$ for $y=f(x)$ given above." :question/worked-solution "To graph the function $y = f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right),$ we take the graph of $y = f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae191c7f3af5c16113d28c20572eba4559b08145327cab651a49ca7d39991502.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115094 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d199ed2c-8188-5d80-9bb6-7b69ae684747" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Graph of a Horizontally Compressed Function"}] :question/id #uuid "9da96071-1bed-5e17-9d59-45948a0aaa5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4984" :question/problem "The graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) is shown below. Sketch the graph of ![](images/92/928f63764afcdf6f3d6b2544ea46c4ea9766984bf6c419024b280a1144fa879f.svg)\n\n![](images/0d/0d6473127647437b8f45b6a29713710534c9d36b4cc8be544be4f0a0a91a51bd.png)" :question/worked-solution "To graph the function ![](images/85/85749f0cf7d11e38e0842d75182adae674cbc66a0ae756723093c3d0fc789c86.svg) we take the graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) and compress it by factor of ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) parallel to the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis. This has the effect of dividing the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinates of all points on ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) by ![](images/9f/9fc073d1e6ebde82eb949a875e40be00b9196e9b14473ee13fa47a26a98d8450.svg)\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/42/42b655b11d17c7c4f6afd8f33fc048193b6b101dde586c67a6a40635d82c629a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115095 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115266 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03cffed8-0c7f-5175-907c-3285cc07b2ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point that Lies on a Horizontally Stretched Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0063a610-8c9a-587f-bd0a-7296d778059b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4985" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(6,7)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Find a point that must lie on the graph of $y= f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}x\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y = f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}x\\right)$ represents a stretch of the function $y = f(x)$ by a factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(6,7)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $\\left(4\\cdot 6 , 7\\right) = (24,7),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y = f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}x\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d199ed2c-8188-5d80-9bb6-7b69ae684747" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Graph of a Horizontally Compressed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2476165c-38a6-5acc-83b4-68b7d2502fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/22c36a04818dbd58d347acf37bfcf366555691099635bf256362faced8757f74.png" :db/id #edb/ref 1