5e82183699b14fe898b4537e16fc36ad5bf5b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/15/15b9b0530dbd4a193efb2ffd6d7719472c6aa641714298c4644ae47d167cf175.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115266 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03cffed8-0c7f-5175-907c-3285cc07b2ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point that Lies on a Horizontally Stretched Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9af2f624-c49e-5aad-b7ac-1c85f0c67913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9af2f624-c49e-5aad-b7ac-1c85f0c67913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529961} :answer-field/id #uuid "25670d29-e707-557b-851a-0b474254fad5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530246} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbb79cb9-583f-5c86-888e-10bd88765ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbb79cb9-583f-5c86-888e-10bd88765ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530123} :answer-field/id #uuid "b351670c-7194-5c35-a8e5-250770b6c8e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6ba2a58-fad3-5500-859d-8acd4ee3f5d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-249480" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(5,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Find the point $Q$ that must lie on $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x).$\n\nThe coordinates of $Q$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(5,3)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $(4\\cdot 5,3)=(20,3),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115266 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03cffed8-0c7f-5175-907c-3285cc07b2ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point that Lies on a Horizontally Stretched Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31eec92c-4aee-5333-a1f2-267cf91c079f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31eec92c-4aee-5333-a1f2-267cf91c079f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529691} :answer-field/id #uuid "57da7ef3-0c94-50c0-9233-711a1a474bca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530283} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b7b33d-f8d9-53ae-b05e-74b05abed6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30b7b33d-f8d9-53ae-b05e-74b05abed6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529687} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b7c184-2a67-5c73-afc6-a80fafa63c8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bebb2ef-00e1-59ff-ace6-82ab8feb5573" :question/math-academy-id "q-249484" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(2,-5)$ lies on the curve $y=f(7x).$ Find the point $Q$ that must lie on $y=f(x).$\n\nThe coordinates of $Q$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)=f(\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (7x)),$ the function $y=f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(7x)$ by a factor of $7$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(7x)$ by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,-5)$ on $f(7x)$ is mapped to the point $(7\\cdot 2,-5)=(14,-5),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115266 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03cffed8-0c7f-5175-907c-3285cc07b2ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point that Lies on a Horizontally Stretched Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ef32a63-bde3-5142-b050-b6ddd35472ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ef32a63-bde3-5142-b050-b6ddd35472ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529974} :answer-field/id #uuid "30cb0c6b-7211-5811-94b8-3438d0ab648e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4f1599b-6806-5734-b494-6d3d1686f10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4f1599b-6806-5734-b494-6d3d1686f10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530032} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb4cdc89-f0b0-5651-9006-97d7c5f0bbed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4862c2b5-a520-5c18-b89e-ce5de629850e" :question/math-academy-id "q-249487" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-15,6)$ lies on the curve $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$ Find the point $Q$ that must lie on $y=f(x).$\n\nThe coordinates of $Q$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)=f(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}x)),$ the function $y=f(x)$ represents a compression of the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-15,6)$ on $f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ is mapped to the point $(\\frac{-15}{3},6)=(-5,6),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d199ed2c-8188-5d80-9bb6-7b69ae684747" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Graph of a Horizontally Compressed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41f73ff2-bb51-560b-b2d9-d30ed19b4108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "imag